      "Only a couple of flavors, with 2-3 g of sugar per scoop",
      "Not an option if your baby's doctor has asked you to avoid dairy"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The best-documented dairy protein for nursing parents who want independent testing first.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein per serving": "21 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 100",
      "Third-party testing": "NSF Certified for Sport",
      "Best for": "Certified daily whey"
    }
  },
  {
    "id": "truvani-organic-whey-protein",
    "name": "Truvani Organic Whey Protein Powder, Vanilla",
    "brand": "Truvani",
    "badge": "Best Short-Label Whey",
    "category": "USDA Organic grass-fed whey concentrate",
    "description": "A USDA Organic whey concentrate that Truvani says comes from grass-fed, pasture-raised cows on certified organic farms. The vanilla version has three ingredients: organic whey protein concentrate, organic vanilla powder and organic monk fruit extract. Retailer listings show about 20 g of protein and roughly 100 calories per scoop at the time of writing. It suits parents who want a flavored shake with no artificial sweeteners or sugar alcohols.",
    "pros": [
