    "specs": {
      "Protein per serving": "About 25 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 120",
      "Third-party testing": "None we could confirm",
      "Best for": "DIY smoothies on a budget"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Protein Powder for Breastfeeding",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-protein-powder-for-breastfeeding/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it safe to drink protein shakes while breastfeeding?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most nursing parents, a plain, third-party tested protein powder is considered fine, but check with your doctor, especially if you take medication. The protein itself is ordinary food. The things to avoid are contaminants such as lead, stimulants like caffeine and green tea extract, and unstudied herbal blends. Choose a short label with independent testing and keep it to about one serving a day."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How much protein do I need while breastfeeding?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The Institute of Medicine sets the lactation RDA at 1.1 g of protein per kilogram of body weight, about 71 g a day for a reference woman, compared with about 46 g for women who are not pregnant or nursing. Some parents, especially those who are active or recovering from a cesarean birth, may benefit from a little more. A dietitian can set a personal target."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does protein powder increase milk supply?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "No. There is no good evidence that protein powder raises milk supply in well-nourished parents. Supply depends mainly on frequent, effective milk removal. Eating enough calories and protein supports your health and energy while you nurse, but a shake is not a galactagogue. If you are worried about supply, an IBCLC lactation consultant can find the cause."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does fenugreek actually work for breast milk?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The evidence is weak. Small trials report modest increases in milk volume, but a 2020 Cochrane review rated the overall certainty as low, and the Academy of Breastfeeding Medicine does not recommend routine use of galactagogues. Fenugreek can cause digestive upset, a maple-syrup body odor and lower blood sugar, and it may trigger reactions in people allergic to peanuts or chickpeas."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best protein powder for breastfeeding moms?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Our top pick is Ritual's Pregnancy and Postpartum shake, which gives 20 g of pea protein and 250 mg of choline per serving per its label and contains no lactation herbs. Huel Black Edition is better if you need calories as well as protein, Thorne Whey Isolate is the best certified dairy option, and Premier Protein is the easiest shelf-stable shake for night feeds."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can artificial sweeteners in protein shakes pass into breast milk?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Small amounts can. Studies have detected sucralose and acesulfame potassium in breast milk after mothers consumed them. The FDA considers these sweeteners safe at normal intakes and no harm to infants has been established, but long-term research is limited. If you prefer to avoid them, choose an unsweetened powder like Naked Whey or one sweetened with monk fruit, such as Truvani."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use protein shakes to lose weight while breastfeeding?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They can help as a filling, high-protein snack while you eat slightly less, but go slowly. Lactation experts generally advise gradual weight loss over months and discourage crash diets, particularly in the first weeks while supply is establishing. Do not replace several meals a day with shakes without medical guidance, and keep total calories high enough that you feel well."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Protein Powder",
    tags: [
      "best protein powder for breastfeeding",
      "protein powder while breastfeeding",
      "is protein powder safe while breastfeeding",
      "lactation protein powder",
      "postpartum protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Protein Powder for Breastfeeding</Fragment>
      Seven protein powders and shakes for nursing parents, chosen on protein per serving, calories, independent testing and sweeteners, with an honest look at fenugreek and other lactation add-ins. Talk to your doctor or a lactation consultant before starting any supplement.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#at-a-glance">Comparison at a Glance</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#how-much-protein">Protein While Nursing</a>
      <a href="#calories">Calories and Weight</a>
      <a href="#lactation-add-ins">Fenugreek and Lactation Blends</a>
      <a href="#testing">Testing and Safety</a>
      <a href="#sweeteners">Sweeteners and Dairy</a>
      <a href="#newborn-days">Using It With a Newborn</a>
      <a href="#get-help">When to Get Help</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best protein powder for breastfeeding gives you protein and, when you need it, calories, without herbs or additives that you then have to wonder about every time the baby is fussy. Ritual's Pregnancy and Postpartum shake is our top pick: 20 g of pea protein, 250 mg of choline and no lactation herbs, per its label. Huel Black Edition is the choice when you are missing whole meals and need about 400 calories in a glass. Thorne Whey Protein Isolate is the best certified dairy option, and Premier Protein is the easiest one-handed shake for night feeds. A protein powder will not increase your milk supply, and the evidence for lactation blends such as fenugreek is weak. This is not medical advice; if supply is a worry, see an IBCLC lactation consultant or your doctor.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="at-a-glance" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Breastfeeding Protein Powders Compared at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Here is every pick side by side. Protein, calories and sugar are per serving from the label or retailer listings at the time of writing and vary by flavor. Price tier compares cost per serving across this list.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein/scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sugar</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Price tier</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Certification</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Ritual Pregnancy & Postpartum</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">115-120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">0 g added</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Premium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Testing marks listed by brand</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Huel Black Edition</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 40 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 400</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Varies by flavor</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Thorne Whey Isolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2-3 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Premium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">NSF Certified for Sport</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Truvani Organic Whey</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Low; check label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Mid</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">USDA Organic</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Premier Protein Shake</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 160</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Orgain Organic Plant</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 150</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Under 1 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">USDA Organic</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Naked Whey (unflavored)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2 g (milk)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Budget</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None confirmed</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Two questions decide which row is yours. First, do you mainly need protein, or are you so short on time that you are missing meals and need calories too? If it is the second, Huel is in a different category from the rest. Second, can you have dairy? If your baby's doctor has suggested cutting cow's milk, Ritual, Huel and Orgain are the dairy-free options. For general picks beyond the postpartum months, see our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-women/">best protein powder for women</a> guide.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Best Protein Powders for Breastfeeding
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these on the label and on public certification records, not on hands-on testing: protein and calories per serving, independent testing, sweeteners and additives, how easy each is to use with a baby in one arm, and price tier at the time of writing. We did not test these products ourselves. Figures come from the brand's label or retailer listings and vary by flavor, so check the panel on the product you buy. Our methods are explained on the <a href="/how-we-test/">how we test</a> page.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="how-much-protein" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much Protein Do You Need While Breastfeeding?
          </h2>

          <p>
            The Institute of Medicine's Dietary Reference Intakes set the protein RDA for lactation at 1.1 g per kilogram of body weight per day, about 71 g for a reference woman. That is the same figure as late pregnancy and roughly 25 g a day more than the 46 g, or 0.8 g per kilogram, recommended for adult women who are not pregnant or nursing. The increase covers the protein that goes into breast milk, which works out to several grams a day for a baby who is exclusively breastfed.
          </p>

          <p>
            The research base for lactation is thinner than for pregnancy, and the RDA is a floor for meeting basic needs rather than an optimum. If you are also training, recovering from a cesarean birth or trying to lose weight gradually, a target somewhere between 1.1 and 1.3 g per kilogram is a reasonable conversation to have with a dietitian. For a 70 kg woman, that is roughly 77-91 g a day. Our <a href="/protein-calculator/">protein calculator</a> will give you a personal starting figure.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most of that should come from meals. Eggs, Greek yogurt, milk, fish, chicken, beans, lentils and tofu all bring other nutrients lactation needs, such as iodine, iron, choline and omega-3 fats. Fish is particularly useful for DHA, and the FDA and EPA advise choosing two to three servings a week of lower-mercury fish while breastfeeding. A protein shake supplies none of that unless it is fortified.
          </p>

          <p>
            A powder earns its place in the gaps. The first weeks with a newborn are when meals disappear: you eat crackers standing up, forget lunch entirely, and realize at 4 p.m. that you have had coffee and a banana. A shake that delivers 20-30 g of protein in thirty seconds is an easy way to keep your intake from collapsing during that stretch. Our explainer on <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> covers the per-kilogram math for every life stage.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Lactation by the Numbers</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">71 g</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Protein RDA per day (IOM)</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">+330</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Extra calories per day, months 0-6</p>
              </div>
